EXT. SALT FLAT - MIDDAY

White to every horizon. The heat doesn't shimmer so much as hum. A pickup truck lies on its side, one wheel still turning, as if the world hadn't told it to stop.

Three figures sit in the narrow shadow of the chassis, barely a hand's width of shade.

CORAZÓN BELLAMY (50s, geologist, wide hat, lips cracked white) holds a canteen to the light and shakes it. Almost nothing sloshes.

JUNIOR AKINTOLA (20s, driver, one sleeve torn off and wrapped around a swollen wrist) rocks in place, breathing through his teeth.

OLD DASH, a woman of seventy known to everyone as DASH (rancher's daughter, braided silver hair, eyes narrowed to cracks), cuts a strip from her shirt with a pocketknife and does not look up.

JUNIOR
It's six miles.

DASH
It's nine.

CORAZÓN
It's eleven if we drift north.

JUNIOR
We can do eleven.

DASH
You can't do three.

He opens his mouth. Closes it. The wrapped wrist twitches against his chest.

CORAZÓN
We wait for dusk.

JUNIOR
Dusk, we're dead.

DASH
Dusk, we're alive and walking.

JUNIOR
Dusk, nobody comes. Nobody even knows.

CORAZÓN
Somebody knows.

A silence in which the metal ticks. Dash ties the strip around his wrist in a tight, practical knot. He yelps. She pats the knot twice, as if it were a dog.

A sound. High, thin. Not wind. Not metal.

All three go still. The sound again, rising. A pinprick of glare on the horizon, moving.

JUNIOR
(whispers)
Is that...

CORAZÓN
Don't.

DASH
Mirror. Corazón, your mirror.

Corazón scrabbles in her vest. Pulls out a cracked compact. Flips it open. Catches the sun and throws a white coin of light across the flat.

JUNIOR
Left. Left, left!

CORAZÓN
I know!

DASH
Slow. Don't flash it. Hold it steady.

The glare grows. A quad bike, kicking a plume of white. It slows. Stops fifty yards off. A hand rises from the rider.

Junior breaks. Laughs, a wet cracked sound. Slides down against the wheel and lets his head fall back.

Corazón lowers the mirror. Her hand is shaking now, finally. Dash lays her palm over it, firm, and holds it there.

DASH (CONT'D)
Good girl.

CORAZÓN
Don't call me that.

DASH
(almost a smile)
Drink.

She passes the canteen. Corazón drinks the last of it. Hands it to Junior first.

FADE OUT.
